    "two pointers"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "grind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "both lists are already sorted - do you really need to sort anything?",
    "what if you looked at just the front of each list and always picked the smaller one?",
    "keep an index into each list, compare the two current values, append the smaller and advance that index",
    "once one list runs out, the rest of the other list is already sorted, so just append what's left"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting to append the remaining elements once one list is exhausted",
    "using strict less-than instead of handling ties consistently, which can silently drop equal elements",
    "not handling the case where one input list is empty from the start"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given two singly linked lists, `list1` and `list2`, each\nrepresented as an array of their node values in order and each already\nsorted in non-decreasing order.\n\n**Merge them into a single sorted list and return its values in order.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= list1.length <= 50",
      "0 <= list2.length <= 50",
      "-100 <= list1[i], list2[i] <= 100",
      "both lists are already sorted in non-decreasing order"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "one or both lists are empty",
      "lists of very different lengths",
      "duplicate values across the two lists"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "list1 = [1, 2, 4], list2 = [1, 3, 4]",
        "output": "[1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4]",
        "explanation": "merging the two sorted lists keeps everything sorted"
